---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PdfToText from '@/components/tools/pdf-convert/PdfToText.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="pdf-to-text">
  <PdfToText client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to extract text from a PDF</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop the PDF onto the box above. It is parsed by pdf.js in this tab; nothing is uploaded.</li>
      <li>Wait for the progress bar. Extraction runs page by page and stays responsive on long documents.</li>
      <li>Review the per-page output. Each page has its own copy button so you can take just one section.</li>
      <li>Toggle the page separators on or off depending on whether you want <code>--- Page 3 ---</code> markers in the file.</li>
      <li>Copy everything, or download a UTF-8 <code>.txt</code> file.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Two kinds of PDF, and why it matters</h2>
    <p>
      Every PDF falls into one of two categories, and the difference decides
      whether text extraction is possible at all.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th></th>
            <th>Text-based PDF</th>
            <th>Scanned / image-only PDF</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Made by</td>
            <td>Word, LaTeX, browsers, InDesign, "Save as PDF"</td>
            <td>Scanners, phone camera apps, fax gateways</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Contains</td>
            <td>Real characters plus font and position data</td>
            <td>One photograph per page</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Can you select text in a reader?</td>
            <td>Yes</td>
            <td>No</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Works with this tool?</td>
            <td>Yes, exactly</td>
            <td>No. The tool warns you and does not return an empty file</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>What you need instead</td>
            <td>Nothing</td>
            <td>OCR software, for example your scanner's own OCR</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      A quick check before you start: open the PDF in any reader and try to select
      a word with the mouse. If the selection highlights letters, this tool will
      give you clean text. If it draws a rectangle over the whole page, the page is
      an image.
    </p>

    <h2>How the reading order is reconstructed</h2>
    <p>
      This is the part most extractors get wrong, so it is worth explaining. A PDF
      does not store paragraphs, sentences or even lines. It stores instructions
      like "draw these glyphs at this position with this font". pdf.js hands back
      those positioned runs in whatever order the generator wrote them, which is
      frequently not reading order.
    </p>
    <p>To turn that back into prose, the tool:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>groups glyph runs into lines by comparing their baselines, with a tolerance scaled to the font size;</li>
      <li>sorts the lines from the top of the page downwards, and each line's fragments from left to right;</li>
      <li>inserts a space where two fragments are further apart than the font's natural letter spacing, which recovers word gaps that the PDF stored as positioning rather than space characters;</li>
      <li>measures the typical gap between lines, then starts a new paragraph whenever the gap is noticeably larger, the line is indented, or it begins with a bullet or a number;</li>
      <li>re-joins words split across a line break by a hyphen.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The honest caveat: sorting by vertical position is correct for single-column
      pages and wrong for magazines, newspapers and academic two-column layouts,
      where it interleaves the columns. Tables come out as rows of words without
      their column structure. No purely geometric approach avoids this, and any
      tool that claims otherwise is guessing too.
    </p>

    <h2>Useful things to do with the output</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Search and quote</strong> a long report without scrolling through a viewer.</li>
      <li><strong>Count words</strong> in a manuscript or a submission with a defined limit.</li>
      <li><strong>Feed a language model or script</strong> that expects plain text rather than a binary format.</li>
      <li><strong>Diff two versions</strong> of a contract by extracting both and comparing the text.</li>
      <li><strong>Rescue content</strong> from a PDF whose source document has been lost.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If you want an editable document rather than plain text, the
      <a href="/pdf-to-word">PDF to Word converter</a> wraps this same extraction in
      a real <code>.docx</code>. To go the other way, see
      <a href="/text-to-pdf">Text to PDF</a>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
